{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbjT1b6LfujBkzBWgpit", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The main difference is that a **list is mutable** and a **tuple is immutable**.\n\n| | List | Tuple |\n|---|---|---|\n| Syntax | `[1, 2, 3]` | `(1, 2, 3)` |\n| Mutable | Yes: you can add, remove or change items | No: it can't change after creation |\n| Hashable | No, so it can't be a dict key or set member | Yes, if all its items are hashable |\n| Methods | Many (`append`, `remove`, `sort`, ...) | Only `count` and `index` |\n| Memory and speed | Slightly more overhead | Slightly smaller and faster |\n\n```python\nnums = [1, 2, 3]\nnums[0] = 99          # fine\nnums.append(4)        # fine\n\npoint = (1, 2)\npoint[0] = 99         # TypeError: 'tuple' object does not support item assignment\n```\n\n**When to use which**\n- **List:** a collection that grows, shrinks or gets modified, usually of items of the same kind (a list of users, for example).\n- **Tuple:** a fixed record where position has meaning, such as `(x, y)`, `(name, age)` or a function returning several values. Tuples are also what you want for dict keys, e.g. `{(0, 0): \"origin\"}`.\n\n**Caveat:** a tuple is only shallowly immutable. If it contains a mutable object, that object can still change:\n\n```python\nt = ([1, 2], 3)\nt[0].append(99)       # works: t is now ([1, 2, 99], 3)\n```\n\n**Gotcha:** a single-item tuple needs a trailing comma. `(5,)` is a tuple, but `(5)` is just the integer 5."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7205, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16780, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7205}, "output_tokens": 1, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "pa